Robert Sarver announced Wednesday he's  begun the process of selling off his two  professional basketball teams the NBA's  Phoenix Suns and the wnba's Phoenix  Mercury Sarver who owns about 35 percent  of the sons purchased the team in 2004  for 401 million dollars the sons will  likely fetch at least 2.5 billion  dollars according to sports Bankers  contacted by Forbes a year ago Forbes  valued the Suns at 1.8 billion dollars  18th out of the League's 30 teams Sarver  set his ownership of the teams was no  longer possible after an MBA  investigation found he used racist  language and fostered a hostile work  environment the 10-month MBA  investigation found Sarver used the  m-word on at least three occasions while  quoting others bullied employees and  made sexually charged comments to and  about women the probe was launched after  ESPN published a report last year  detailing the allegations which Sarver  denied at the time  his punishment which required him to  stay away from both teams operations for  a year but didn't require him to sell  either of them was slammed by LeBron  James and other players and officials  while the Sun's Vice chairman John  najafi the team's second largest  shareholder called for Sarver to step  down Sarver said he initially planned to  use the suspension to make amends but  after backlash to his suspension Sarver  realized quote whatever good I have done  or could still do is outweighed by the  things I have said in the past  [Music]  foreign
 

